-- F-ge1: the chapeau names "any act of terrorism" where the list it introduces makes sense
-- only as "any of the following", and the clause's own later sentence treats (a) to (c) as
-- heads ("items (a) to (c) above"). Taken: (a), (b), (c) and the suppression limb are each
-- an exclusion in its own right. Reading (i), not taken, needs a connection with terrorism
-- before anything is excluded; under Law 08/2022/QH15 art 24 (aid, lines 588-591)
-- an unclear term is read for the policyholder, so (i) is open to the Insured and would leave
-- war damage without terrorism uncovered by this exclusion (finding X-war). Limb (d), "an excluded
-- risk", adds nothing: a loss from an excluded risk is excluded already.
@ref General Exclusion 1, src:687-719 (fork F-ge1, reading (ii))
GIVEN circumstances IS A LIST OF `A circumstance of a loss`
GIVETH A LIST OF `A General Exclusion`
`General Exclusion 1 applies to` circumstances MEANS
    `those that hold among`
        (LIST PAIR `General Exclusion 1(a): war, invasion, hostilities or civil war` (`it was` `war, invasion, foreign enemy, hostilities or civil war`)
            , PAIR `General Exclusion 1(b): an act of terrorism`                     (`it was` `an act of terrorism`)
            , PAIR `General Exclusion 1(c): mutiny, rising, rebellion, usurped power, martial law or siege`
                   (`it was` `mutiny, popular rising, insurrection, rebellion, usurped power, martial law or siege`)
            , PAIR `General Exclusion 1: action taken to control, prevent or suppress (a) to (c)`
                   (`it was` `action taken to control, prevent or suppress war, terrorism or rebellion`))
    WHERE
        `it was` c MEANS elem c circumstances

-- F-ge1, reading (i), not taken: nothing is excluded without a connection to terrorism.
@ref F-ge1, reading (i), not taken
GIVEN circumstances IS A LIST OF `A circumstance of a loss`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`on reading (i), General Exclusion 1 excludes` circumstances MEANS
    elem `an act of terrorism` circumstances

